.TH SNAILSYNK 1 "2026" "SnailSynk" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
snailsynk \- share files, text and notes on your own network
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snailsynk
[\fB\-\-setup\fR] [\fB\-\-reset\-admin\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-browser\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
SnailSynk is a small web server for sharing files, a text buffer and notes between the devices on a trusted
network. It serves HTTPS with a self\-signed certificate (default port 9000) and opens the page in the browser.
.PP
The first run asks for an admin account, the port and an optional Gemini API key. Settings and data are kept in
\fI~/.local/share/snailsynk\fR (or \fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/snailsynk\fR); uploaded files go to \fI~/Downloads/SnailSynk/files\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-setup
Run the setup questions again.
.TP
.B \-\-reset\-admin
Choose a new admin username and password.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-browser
Do not open the browser.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SNAILSYNK_HOME
Use this folder for settings and data instead of the default.
.TP
.B SNAILSYNK_PORT
Port to listen on (normally set in the .env file in the data folder).
.SH SECURITY
Anyone on the network can upload and use the text buffer without logging in. Do not expose SnailSynk to the
open internet.
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/keshrisaksham/SnailSynk
